Abstract

This dissertation constructs a complete and self‑contained mathematical theory of Resonant Physics‑Native Intelligence (RPNI). The central premise is that adaptive, temporally structured intelligence can emerge spontaneously from the collective synchronisation dynamics of coupled nonlinear oscillators, without any reference to static weights, backpropagation, or discrete-time computation. The fundamental computational unit is the Oscillatory Cognitive Unit (OCU), a limit‑cycle element described by the Stuart–Landau normal form. Networks of OCUs interact through adaptive, time‑varying, and generally non‑reciprocal couplings that evolve according to a three‑factor Hebbian learning rule gated by globally estimated susceptibility and critical branching signals. This plasticity drives the network toward a self‑organised Critical Resonant State (CRS), where the macroscopic coherence susceptibility is maximal, the largest Lyapunov exponent vanishes, and the avalanche size distribution follows a critical power law.

The thesis provides rigorous proofs for all major claims.

1. The convergence of the adaptive learning rule is proved using a novel two‑timescale stochastic approximation analysis over the full non‑compact Stuart–Landau phase–amplitude space. Uniform exponential ergodicity is established via an explicit Foster–Lyapunov condition and a local Doeblin minorisation, and the slow coupling variables are shown to converge almost surely to the stationary points of an averaged objective by the recent extensions of Borkar’s scheme to non‑compact spaces with Markovian noise (Mahadevan et al., 2025; Bhatnagar, 2025). Finite‑time error bounds are also derived.
2. The three indicators of criticality—maximal susceptibility, neutral Lyapunov stability, and critical branching—are proved to coincide at the synchronisation transition up to finite‑size corrections. Precise scaling laws K_\chi(N)=K_c+O(N^{-2/5}), K_\lambda(N)=K_c+O(N^{-1/3}), and K_\sigma(N)=K_c+O(N^{-1/2}) are obtained from deterministic sampling, random matrix theory, and branching process mappings, respectively.
3. Memory is modelled as a set of dynamical attractors, and their stability under non‑reciprocal and delayed coupling is guaranteed through a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional and a weighted Riemannian contraction metric, with explicit thresholds on connection strength and delay derived from recent non‑reciprocal oscillator analyses.
4. Storage capacity is raised to O(N^2) by exploiting cross‑frequency phase–amplitude coupling and torus bifurcations; the retrieval cross‑talk is bounded by O(\sqrt{P/N}) using a Gardner‑style mean‑field calculation.

A complete formal architecture of the RPNI machine is specified, and all mathematical guarantees are summarised. The work is entirely theoretical; no numerical experiments or physical implementations are performed. Its contribution is a verified mathematical blueprint for a new class of physics‑native, temporally adept intelligent systems.

