1. Introduction

Contemporary machine intelligence is built upon the optimisation of static weight matrices in deep neural networks, trained by global backpropagation. While this paradigm has achieved remarkable results, it is fundamentally a digital abstraction, disconnected from the physical constraints of time, energy, and real‑world continuous dynamics. Biological nervous systems, in contrast, are intrinsically oscillatory: neuronal populations synchronise in the theta, alpha, and gamma bands, exhibit cross‑frequency coupling, and operate near a critical state where information transmission and dynamical range are maximised (Beggs & Plenz, 2003; Shew et al., 2009). This thesis adopts the position that intelligence can be mathematically re‑founded as an emergent property of adaptive, resonant oscillator networks, without any reliance on symbolic computation, fixed connectivity, or externally imposed error signals.

The programme builds directly upon the Phasor Agents architecture (2026, arXiv:2601.04362), which demonstrated for the first time that a network of Stuart–Landau oscillators with eligibility‑trace plasticity and global modulatory signals can learn to perform temporal tasks. Our contribution is to extend Phasor Agents into a complete and rigorously proved mathematical framework. We go beyond numerical validation and provide convergence guarantees, a precise definition of a unified critical state, stability proofs for non‑reciprocal memory, and scalable capacity results.

The core primitive is the Oscillatory Cognitive Unit (OCU), a limit‑cycle oscillator described by the Stuart–Landau normal form,

\frac{d z_i}{d t} = (\mu_i + i\omega_i)z_i - (\beta_i + i\gamma_i)|z_i|^2 z_i + \sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}(t)\,\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j) + I_i^{\text{ext}}(t),

where z_i\in\mathbb{C}, \mu_i,\beta_i>0, \omega_i\sim g(\omega), and the coupling function \mathcal{C} is bounded and Lipschitz. The coupling weights K_{ij}(t) evolve on a slow timescale according to a synchronisation‑gated Hebbian rule, so learning, memory, and computation are not programmed but arise from the evolution of phases, amplitudes, and interactions.

This dissertation advances the Phasor Agents framework in four critical directions, each backed by rigorous proof:

1. Two‑timescale convergence of the full adaptive Stuart–Landau system with Markovian noise, using the latest extensions of stochastic approximation for non‑compact spaces (Mahadevan et al., 2025; Bhatnagar, 2025). We prove almost‐sure convergence of the coupling matrix to the set of stationary points of an effective Lyapunov function, under verifiable uniform ergodicity conditions.
2. A unified Critical Resonant State defined by the simultaneous satisfaction of maximal susceptibility, zero largest Lyapunov exponent, and critical branching (\sigma_b=1). We prove that these three conditions coincide at the synchronisation transition, and we quantify their finite‑N separation through explicit scaling laws derived from deterministic sampling (Coletta et al., 2024, arXiv:2406.18904), random matrix theory, and branching process mappings.
3. Robust memory attractors under non‑reciprocal and delayed coupling, established via a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional and a contraction metric. We provide explicit thresholds on coupling strength and delay that guarantee global asymptotic stability, thereby ruling out the metastable switching observed in recent non‑reciprocal Kuramoto studies (2025–2026).
4. Enhanced storage capacity to O(N^2) using a two‑band architecture with cross‑frequency phase–amplitude coupling and torus bifurcations. A Gardner‑style mean‑field analysis yields exact cross‑talk bounds and proves that the retrieval noise remains O(\sqrt{P/N}).

The thesis is purely mathematical: no simulations or hardware experiments are performed. Its objective is to produce a formal, analytically tractable, and fully verified substrate for future physical implementations in, e.g., photonic, spintronic, or memristive hardware.

