2. Mathematical Foundations of Oscillatory Cognitive Units

2.1 The OCU: Stuart–Landau Dynamics with Adaptive Coupling

The state of the i-th OCU is a complex variable z_i(t)\in\mathbb{C}, i=1,\dots,N. Its intrinsic dynamics are given by the Stuart–Landau (SL) normal form, which is the universal unfolding of a supercritical Hopf bifurcation and thus captures the essential phenomenology of any system near the onset of self‑sustained oscillations (Kuramoto, 1984). The full adaptive network obeys

\frac{d z_i}{d t} = (\mu_i + i\omega_i)z_i - (\beta_i + i\gamma_i)|z_i|^2 z_i + \sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}(t)\,\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j) + I_i^{\text{ext}}(t) + \sqrt{2D}\,\xi_i(t),
\tag{2.1}

where the term \sqrt{2D}\,\xi_i(t) is additive complex white noise with independent real and imaginary parts, each a standard real Wiener process of intensity D>0. The parameters satisfy \mu_i,\beta_i>0 and \gamma_i\in\mathbb{R}; the intrinsic frequencies \omega_i are independent samples from a probability density g(\omega) that is Lipschitz, symmetric about zero, and has compact support.

The coupling function \mathcal{C} is chosen to be bounded and globally Lipschitz to guarantee global existence of solutions for all time, even when the connections K_{ij}(t) evolve slowly. We adopt

\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j) = \frac{z_j - z_i}{|z_j - z_i|}\,\tanh(|z_j - z_i|)
\tag{2.2}

with the convention \mathcal{C}(z,z)=0. It is easy to verify that |\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j)|\le 1 and that \mathcal{C} is C^1 except at z_i=z_j, where it can be smoothed further without loss of Lipschitz continuity.

Proposition 2.1 (Global existence and uniqueness).
For any initial conditions z_i(0)\in\mathbb{C} and any continuous adaptation of K_{ij}(t) with |K_{ij}(t)|\le K_{\max}, the system (2.1) admits a unique global strong solution. The proof follows from standard stochastic differential equation theory: the drift is locally Lipschitz and satisfies a one‑sided linear growth condition, while the noise is additive and non‑degenerate. The cubic term -|z_i|^2 z_i provides a global dissipative restoring force, ensuring no explosion in finite time.

2.2 Macroscopic Order Parameter and Susceptibility

Collective behaviour is quantified by the complex order parameter

R(t)\,e^{i\psi(t)} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^N e^{i\theta_j(t)},\qquad R\in[0,1],
\tag{2.3}

where \theta_i=\arg(z_i) when |z_i|>0; for vanishing amplitude we assign a uniformly random phase. The static susceptibility \chi measures how the mean coherence responds to a uniform change in coupling strength. Defining the effective mean coupling K_{\text{eff}} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i,j}K_{ij},

\chi = \frac{\partial R}{\partial K_{\text{eff}}}.
\tag{2.4}

In the phase‑reduced limit (Section 2.3) with additive Gaussian noise of intensity D, the fluctuation–dissipation theorem (FDT) provides an exact relationship (Kuramoto, 1984; Daido, 1990):

\chi = \frac{N}{2D}\,\text{Var}(R),
\tag{2.5}

where \text{Var}(R) is the variance of the order parameter under the stationary distribution. This relation allows us to estimate the instantaneous susceptibility online from a moving window of R(t):

\widehat{\chi}(t) = \frac{N}{2D\,T_{\text{win}}}\int_{t-T_{\text{win}}}^t \bigl(R(s)-\bar{R}(t)\bigr)^2\,ds,
\tag{2.6}

with \bar{R}(t) the moving average. When the system operates near the synchronisation transition, the FDT holds robustly even for the full SL dynamics because amplitudes remain nearly constant.

For the Lorentzian frequency distribution g(\omega)=\frac{\Delta}{\pi(\omega^2+\Delta^2)}, the thermodynamic limit (N\to\infty) gives the classic Kuramoto transition at K_c^\infty = 2\Delta. Finite‑size corrections have recently been obtained to high precision by the deterministic sampling method of Coletta, Park, and collaborators (arXiv:2406.18904). Their analysis shows

\chi_{\max} = \frac{0.15}{\Delta}\,N^{2/5} + o(N^{2/5}),\qquad
K_\chi(N) = 2\Delta - 0.8\Delta\,N^{-2/5} + o(N^{-2/5}).
\tag{2.7}

These scaling laws will serve as a key input for the criticality coincidence theorem of Section 4.

2.3 Phase Reduction and the Kuramoto–Sakaguchi Model

In the weak‑coupling limit, where all |K_{ij}|\ll \min_i\mu_i and the noise is small, amplitude fluctuations decay rapidly and one can reduce the dynamics to the phase variables alone. Writing z_i = r_i e^{i\theta_i}, standard averaging yields

\frac{d\theta_i}{dt} = \omega_i + \sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}\,\sin(\theta_j-\theta_i) + I_i^\theta(t) + \sqrt{2D}\,\eta_i(t),
\tag{2.8}

where \eta_i(t) are independent Gaussian white noises, and I_i^\theta(t) = \Im(I_i^{\text{ext}} e^{-i\theta_i}). This is the Kuramoto–Sakaguchi phase model. The reduction is rigorously justified for large timescale separations using geometric singular perturbation theory; we will freely invoke it for analytical tractability in criticality and memory analyses, while the full SL model is retained for the convergence proofs.

2.4 Noise Model and Uniform Exponential Ergodicity

The additive noise term in (2.1) is essential for several reasons: it ensures a smooth invariant measure, enables the fluctuation–dissipation estimator, and—most critically for the convergence theorem—guarantees that the fast dynamics is uniformly exponentially ergodic. We now state and prove this property.

Consider the fixed‑weight system with couplings frozen at an admissible matrix K satisfying |K_{ij}|\le K_{\max}. The state vector \mathbf{z}=(z_1,\dots,z_N)\in\mathbb{C}^N evolves according to (2.1) with K_{ij}(t)\equiv K_{ij} and no external input. We claim that the process possesses a unique invariant probability measure \pi_K, and that there exist constants C,\rho>0, independent of K within the admissible set, such that for any test function \phi with \|\phi\|_{\text{Lip}}\le 1,

\bigl| \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}}[\phi(\mathbf{z}(t))] - \pi_K(\phi) \bigr| \le C\,e^{-\rho t}.
\tag{2.9}

Proof sketch.
We construct a Foster–Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{z}) = \frac12\sum_{i=1}^N |z_i|^2. Computing its infinitesimal generator \mathcal{L} for the SDE (2.1) gives

\mathcal{L}V = \sum_{i=1}^N \Bigl[ \mu_i|z_i|^2 - \beta_i|z_i|^4 + \gamma_i\,\Im(\bar{z}_i|z_i|^2 z_i) + \Re\Bigl(\bar{z}_i\sum_j K_{ij}\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j)\Bigr) \Bigr] + N D.

The \gamma_i term vanishes because \Im(|z_i|^4)=0. Using the boundedness |\mathcal{C}|\le 1 and Young’s inequality, the cross term is bounded by \frac12\sum_{i,j}|K_{ij}|(|z_i|^2+|z_j|^2). With |K_{ij}|\le K_{\max},

\mathcal{L}V \le -\sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\beta_i}{2}|z_i|^4 + C_1(N,K_{\max}), 

where the quadratic terms either cancel or are absorbed into the quartic ones for large |z_i|. Specifically, for |z_i| large enough, say |z_i|^2 > R_0(N,K_{\max},\mu_i,\beta_i), the quartic term dominates and \mathcal{L}V \le -c V^2 + \text{const}. Therefore, the process returns to the compact set \{\mathbf{z}: V(\mathbf{z})\le M\} exponentially fast, and on that compact set the non‑degenerate additive noise ensures a uniform minorisation (local Doeblin) condition. By the classical criteria of Down, Meyn, and Tweedie (1995) and the Bakry–Émery calculus on Riemannian manifolds extended to this non‑compact setting, we obtain a uniform Poincaré inequality for the generator: there exists \rho>0 (dependent on D, K_{\max}, and the system parameters but uniform for all admissible K) such that

\rho\,\text{Var}_{\pi_K}(\phi) \le \mathcal{E}_K(\phi,\phi)

for any smooth \phi, where \mathcal{E}_K is the Dirichlet form. This inequality is equivalent to an L^2 spectral gap, which in turn yields the exponential convergence (2.9) (see Bakry, Gentil, Ledoux, 2014; Hairer & Mattingly, 2011). The uniformity follows because the Lyapunov function and the noise geometry are independent of the specific K_{ij}, and the admissible set of couplings is compact. ∎

This uniform exponential ergodicity is a cornerstone for the stochastic approximation analysis of Section 3.

