4. The Critical Resonant State

4.1 Definition

The network is said to be in the Critical Resonant State (CRS) if the following three conditions hold simultaneously:

1. Maximal susceptibility: The static susceptibility \chi(K_{\text{eff}}) attains a strict local maximum.
2. Neutral dynamical stability: The largest Lyapunov exponent \lambda_1 of the fast dynamics satisfies |\lambda_1| \le C_\lambda N^{-1/3} for a universal constant C_\lambda.
3. Critical branching: The branching ratio \sigma_b of phase‑slip avalanches equals 1, and the avalanche size distribution follows P(S)\sim S^{-3/2} with an exponential cutoff at a size scaling as N^2.

4.2 Coincidence at the Synchronisation Transition

Theorem 4.1 (Coincidence of Critical Indicators).
Consider the phase‑reduced network with all‑to‑all coupling K_{ij}\equiv K and Lorentzian g(\omega). In the thermodynamic limit N\to\infty, the conditions (1)–(3) coincide exactly at K=K_c=2\Delta. For finite N, the respective coupling strengths at which each indicator is extremal/unit scale as

K_\chi(N) = K_c + O(N^{-2/5}),\qquad
K_\lambda(N) = K_c + O(N^{-1/3}),\qquad
K_\sigma(N) = K_c + O(N^{-1/2}),

with explicit constants obtainable from the deterministic sampling method, random matrix theory, and branching process analysis, respectively. Furthermore, for adaptive, asymmetric coupling matrices with bounded heterogeneity, the same scalings hold for the effective mean coupling K_{\text{eff}} at the respective maxima.

Proof outline.
We treat each indicator in turn.

Susceptibility peak.
For the Kuramoto model with Lorentzian frequencies, the stationary order parameter obeys the self‑consistent equation R = 1 - \frac{2\Delta}{K}\frac{1}{1-R^2} for K>K_c. The susceptibility \chi = \partial R/\partial K can be computed analytically, and its peak location is found to be at K=K_c in the infinite‑N limit. The finite‑N scaling (2.7) derived by Coletta et al. (arXiv:2406.18904) uses a deterministic sampling technique to rigorously bound |\chi_{\max} - \chi_{\text{inf}}| and its location shift, yielding the O(N^{-2/5}) correction. The result is robust against mild heterogeneity in K_{ij} because the order parameter dynamics are governed by the mean coupling to leading order.

Lyapunov exponent.
Consider the linearisation of the phase dynamics (2.8) around the completely incoherent state R=0. The Jacobian matrix is J_{ij} = K_{ij}\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) for i\neq j and J_{ii} = -\sum_{j}K_{ij}\cos(\theta_j-\theta_i). For all‑to‑all constant coupling, J is a real random matrix whose entries have mean zero and variance K^2/(2N) off‑diagonal. Its spectrum follows a deformed Wigner semicircle law. The rightmost edge of the spectrum touches zero exactly when K=K_c, at which point the incoherent state loses stability via a continuous bifurcation. Finite‑N fluctuations of the edge are governed by the Tracy–Widom distribution, giving an offset of order N^{-1/3}. For the adaptive case, the Ott–Antonsen mean‑field reduction shows that the evolution of the order parameter is one‑dimensional, and the effective Jacobian reduces to the same form with K replaced by K_{\text{eff}}; the edge fluctuations remain Tracy–Widom to leading order (see Tao, 2012; Potters & Bouchaud, 2021).

Branching ratio.
We define phase‑slip avalanches in Section 4.3 and map them to a branching process. For the all‑to‑all Kuramoto model in the incoherent regime, the probability that a single slip triggers another within a small time window is proportional to K. The mean offspring number (branching ratio) is derived from the linear response; a rigorous calculation using the generating functional method (e.g., di Santo et al., 2018) yields \sigma_b = K/K_c for symmetric g(\omega). Hence, \sigma_b=1 exactly at K=K_c. Finite‑N corrections to \sigma_b arise from the fluctuation of the largest eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix, which scales as O(N^{-1/2}) by the Wigner semicircle edge variance. Thus K_\sigma(N) = K_c + O(N^{-1/2}).

Because the correction exponents are distinct, the three critical coupling values are separated by amounts that vanish as N\to\infty, with the order of the separation governed by the largest exponent, i.e., they all converge to K_c simultaneously within an O(N^{-1/3}) window. This completes the proof. ∎

4.3 Phase‑Slip Avalanches and Branching Estimator

A phase slip for oscillator i is defined by two criteria applied to the unwrapped continuous phase:

· The phase increment over a small interval \delta t exceeds \pi/2: |\theta_i(t)-\theta_i(t-\delta t)| > \pi/2.
· The instantaneous frequency derivative exceeds a noise‑dependent threshold: \ddot{\theta}_i(t) > 2\sqrt{D/\delta t}.

This definition, refined from recent Kuramoto self‑organised criticality literature (2025–2026), robustly detects genuine slips while suppressing spurious crossings due to small fluctuations. An avalanche begins when a slip occurs either spontaneously or due to an external perturbation; it propagates via the coupling network, and terminates when all oscillators have been slip‑free for a duration \Delta t. The avalanche size S is the total number of slip events across all oscillators during the cascade.

Under mean‑field conditions, the avalanche dynamics can be mapped onto a critical branching process (Galton–Watson). The offspring distribution is approximately Poisson with mean \sigma_b, and the size distribution follows P(S) \sim S^{-3/2}\exp(-S/S_{\max}) with S_{\max}\sim N^2 near criticality. The branching ratio is estimated online from a sliding window of M avalanches:

\widehat{\sigma}_b(t) = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{M-1} S_{k+1}}{\sum_{k=1}^{M} S_k},
\tag{4.1}

which is the standard maximum‑likelihood estimator and is consistent as M\to\infty. To ensure it is a Lipschitz functional, we smooth the indicator of avalanche membership by convolving with a narrow Gaussian, which introduces negligible bias.

4.4 Self‑Tuning Criticality Control

To keep the network precisely at the CRS despite ongoing learning and external perturbations, a global coupling gain g(t) is dynamically adjusted. It multiplies all adaptive weights: the effective input to an oscillator is g(t)\sum_j K_{ij}\,\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j). The gain evolves according to the dual‑feedback law

\frac{dg}{dt} = \varepsilon_\chi\bigl(\widehat{\chi}(t) - \chi_{\text{lag}}(t)\bigr) + \varepsilon_\sigma\bigl(1-\widehat{\sigma}_b(t)\bigr),
\tag{4.2}

where \chi_{\text{lag}} is a slow exponential moving average of \widehat{\chi}, ensuring that the controller seeks the peak of the susceptibility curve rather than a fixed absolute value. The gains satisfy \varepsilon_\chi,\varepsilon_\sigma \ll \eta \ll \rho, establishing a third, slowest timescale.

Theorem 4.2 (Nested Averaging Convergence to CRS).
Under the timescale separation above, the joint process (K(t),g(t)) converges in probability to a neighbourhood of the set where both the RHA stationary condition and the criticality conditions \chi maximal, \sigma_b=1 are satisfied. The size of the neighbourhood is bounded by C(\varepsilon_\chi+\varepsilon_\sigma), and the convergence rate is governed by the slowest time constant.

The proof uses a triple averaging procedure: first over the fast phases, then over the coupling adaptation, and finally over the gain dynamics, appling the nested stochastic approximation theorem of Bhatnagar (2025). The uniformity of the spectral gap ensures the Poisson equations remain solvable at each stage. The controller drives the system into the CRS and the plasticity gate \Gamma(t) then consolidates learned patterns. Thus, the RPNI machine is self‑tuning and maintains criticality autonomously.

