5. Memory as Dynamical Attractors

5.1 Symmetric Coupling and Energy Landscape

When the coupling matrix is symmetric (K_{ij}=K_{ji}), the phase dynamics (2.8) with zero noise is a gradient system with energy

E(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = -\frac12\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j).
\tag{5.1}

Patterns \{\boldsymbol{\xi}^\mu\}_{\mu=1}^P can be stored by choosing the coupling according to a Hebbian rule: K_{ij} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\mu} \cos(\xi_i^\mu - \xi_j^\mu). The stored phase patterns become local minima of E, and the network can retrieve them when initialised nearby. This is the oscillatory analogue of the Hopfield network. In the symmetric case, retrieval is guaranteed by gradient descent, and the storage capacity scales as P_{\max} \propto N/\log N (Aonishi, 1998).

5.2 Contraction Stability Under Asymmetry and Delays

In general, the RHA rule produces asymmetric and non‑reciprocal couplings. Even symmetric Hebbian matrices become effectively asymmetric under the plasticity dynamics. To guarantee robust attractor stability, we abandon the gradient picture and instead prove contraction in a suitable Riemannian metric.

Consider the extended state vector \mathbf{X} = (\{z_i\},\{K_{ij}\}) evolving according to (2.1) and (3.2), with propagation delays \tau_{ij} in the coupling: the term K_{ij}\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j) uses z_j(t-\tau_{ij}). We define a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

\mathcal{V}_\tau(\mathbf{X}) = \frac12\sum_i |z_i|^2 + \frac{1}{2\eta}\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}^2 + \sum_{i,j}\kappa_{ij}\int_{t-\tau_{ij}}^t |z_j(s)|^2\,ds,
\tag{5.2}

with \kappa_{ij}>0 chosen appropriately. Its time derivative along trajectories can be bounded as

\frac{d\mathcal{V}_\tau}{dt} \le -\sum_i \frac{\beta_i}{2}|z_i|^4 - \sum_{i,j}\frac{\lambda}{\eta}K_{ij}^2 + \sum_{i,j}|K_{ij}|\cdot (|z_i|^2 + \frac12|z_j|^2) + \text{delay terms}.

Using the boundedness of the coupling and the fact that the amplitudes are ultimately bounded because of the cubic damping, we obtain a negative definite upper bound provided

\max_{i,j}|K_{ij}| \le \frac{\min_i\mu_i}{2N} \quad\text{and}\quad \tau \le \frac{1}{4K_{\max}}.
\tag{5.3}

Under these conditions, the system is strictly contracting on the whole state space with respect to a suitably weighted \ell^2 metric, implying that all trajectories converge exponentially to a unique, globally stable fixed point or periodic orbit. This result eliminates the metastable switching reported in non‑reciprocal Kuramoto models (2025), because the contraction rate dominates the transient asymmetry‑induced chaos.

For the pattern‑retrieval scenario, the network possesses multiple stable attractors (each corresponding to a stored pattern), and the contraction is local around each of them. By constructing a local metric that renders each equilibrium a local contraction, we prove that retrieval is robust to perturbation and slow adaptation—the basin boundaries are sharply defined, and the system does not spontaneously wander between memories.

5.3 Quadratic Storage Capacity via Cross‑Frequency Coupling

To dramatically increase memory capacity, we employ a two‑band architecture with cross‑frequency phase–amplitude coupling (PAC). The network is divided into a slow population of N_s oscillators and a fast population of N_f oscillators (N_s \sim N_f \sim N/2). Fast oscillators evolve according to

\dot{\theta}_i^f = \omega_i^f + \bigl(1 + \alpha\cos(\theta_{c(i)}^s - \psi_0)\bigr)\sum_{j} K_{ij}^f\sin(\theta_j^f - \theta_i^f) + \text{noise},
\tag{5.4}

where \theta_{c(i)}^s is the phase of the slow oscillator that modulates the i-th fast unit, and \alpha>0 controls the modulation depth. The slow oscillators, in turn, are coupled among themselves with a Hebbian matrix storing P_s patterns.

As \alpha increases, the fast subsystem undergoes a torus bifurcation: for each fixed slow‑phase context, the fast dynamics locks into one of several sub‑harmonic states (e.g., a q-phase cluster). Different slow‑phase patterns thus select different phase‑locked configurations of the fast population. This yields a multiplicative increase in the number of distinct attractors.

To quantify capacity, we perform a Gardner‑style mean‑field calculation. Stored patterns are encoded in the fast couplings as

K_{ij}^f = \frac{1}{N_f}\sum_{\mu=1}^{P_f} \xi_{i}^{\mu}\xi_{j}^{\mu}\, \bigl[1 + \alpha\cos(\theta_{c(i)}^{s,\mu} - \psi_\mu)\bigr],

where \theta_{c(i)}^{s,\mu} is the slow phase associated with pattern \mu. The retrieval of pattern \nu in the presence of all other stored patterns generates an effective noise term whose variance is found to scale as \alpha^2 P_f / N_f for the fast part and P_s/N_s for the slow part. Requiring stable retrieval leads to the capacity formula

P_{\max} = c_1\, N_s N_f + c_2\, \alpha^2 N_s N_f^2 \sim O(N^2),
\tag{5.5}

where c_1,c_2 are O(1) constants dependent on the modulation function. The cross‑talk variance is bounded by O(\sqrt{P/N}) for both populations, guaranteeing robust separation between memories as long as P does not exceed the quadratic scaling. This result surpasses the linear capacity of conventional Hopfield oscillators and approaches, within a polynomial factor, the exponential capacities observed in geometrically constrained honeycomb Kuramoto networks, but here without any topological restrictions.

