6. Formal Architecture of the RPNI Machine

The abstract RPNI machine comprises four interconnected modules, all described by continuous‑time differential equations.

6.1 Oscillator Core

The core is a fully connected network of N OCUs governed by (2.1) with dynamically evolving K_{ij}(t). The physical parameter values (\mu_i,\beta_i,\gamma_i,\omega_i) are subject to the constraints described in Section 2. In a hardware realisation, they would be mapped to specific device characteristics (e.g., laser pump currents, spin torque oscillator biases); here they are treated as tunable constants.

6.2 Input Encoding and Separability

External time‑dependent signals \mathbf{u}(t)\in\mathbb{R}^m are injected via input weights W^{\text{in}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times m}:

I_i^{\text{ext}}(t) = \sum_{k=1}^m W_{ik}^{\text{in}} u_k(t).
\tag{6.1}

To maximise the separability of different input sequences, we design W^{\text{in}} such that its columns align with the dominant eigenmodes of the network Jacobian at the critical point. At criticality, the linearised Poincaré map possesses a large number of near‑marginal modes, leading to a high‑rank controllability Gramian. Specifically, let J be the Jacobian of the phase‑amplitude system linearised about the CRS. The input‑state covariance \Sigma_{xu} = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac1T\int_0^T \mathbf{x}(t)\mathbf{u}(t)^\top dt (where \mathbf{x} is a vector of phase deviations) has maximal rank when W^{\text{in}} projects onto the eigenspace of the smallest (near‑zero) Lyapunov exponents. We prove that under this choice, the mutual information between the driving input and the network state is maximised, ensuring that different temporal patterns lead to distinguishable oscillator trajectories.

6.3 Plasticity and Criticality Controller

This module implements (3.1), (3.2), (4.2), and computes the statistics R,\widehat{\chi},\widehat{\sigma}_b in continuous time. All operations are smooth and local, except for the avalanche detection, which requires tracking phase slips across the network. The sliding‑window estimators can be implemented with simple RC filters.

6.4 Readout

The network output y_\ell(t)\in\mathbb{R} is a linear combination of the cosine and sine of the oscillator phases:

y_\ell(t) = \sum_{i=1}^N \bigl( w_{\ell i}\cos\theta_i(t) + v_{\ell i}\sin\theta_i(t) \bigr) + b_\ell.
\tag{6.2}

For theoretical completeness, we note that the optimal readout weights (in the mean‑squared error sense) can be obtained by solving a ridge regression problem on the stationary covariance of the features \phi_i = (\cos\theta_i,\sin\theta_i) under the ergodic invariant measure. Because the system is ergodic (Section 2.4), the covariance matrix is well‑defined and the solution is unique. In any practical instantiation, these weights would be adapted by a separate slow supervised rule, but such adaptation lies outside the purely mathematical scope of the present work.

