Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

This appendix collects the foundational definitions, theorems, and notations used throughout the dissertation. The material is standard; we include it for completeness and to fix notation.

A.1 Stochastic Differential Equations and Ergodicity

Let X_t be the solution of the Itô SDE on \mathbb{R}^d:

dX_t = b(X_t)\,dt + \sigma(X_t)\,dW_t,

with b:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^d, \sigma:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}, and W_t an m-dimensional Brownian motion. The infinitesimal generator is

\mathcal{L} = b\cdot\nabla + \frac12 \operatorname{Tr}(\sigma\sigma^\top \nabla^2).

A probability measure \pi is invariant if \int \mathcal{L}\phi\,d\pi = 0 for all smooth compactly supported \phi. The process is exponentially ergodic if there exist constants C,\rho>0 such that for all sufficiently nice \phi,

\bigl\|P_t\phi - \pi(\phi)\bigr\|_{L^2(\pi)} \le C e^{-\rho t}\|\phi\|_{L^2(\pi)}.

A sufficient condition is a spectral gap for the generator in L^2(\pi), equivalent to a Poincaré inequality: there exists \rho>0 such that

\rho\,\operatorname{Var}_\pi(\phi) \le \mathcal{E}(\phi,\phi),\qquad \mathcal{E}(\phi,\phi) = \int |\sigma^\top\nabla\phi|^2\,d\pi.

Foster–Lyapunov criterion (Meyn & Tweedie, 1993): If there exists a function V\ge 1, compact sets C\subset\mathbb{R}^d, and constants c>0,b<\infty such that \mathcal{L}V \le -c V + b\mathbf{1}_C, then the process has an invariant measure. If additionally a local Doeblin (minorisation) condition holds on C, the process is exponentially ergodic.

A.2 Two‑Timescale Stochastic Approximation with Markovian Noise

Consider the coupled system

dX_t = \epsilon\,h(X_t,Y_t)\,dt,\qquad Y_t \text{ is a Markov process with generator } L_X,

where for each fixed X, Y_t is exponentially ergodic with invariant measure \pi_X. Under uniform ergodicity, the slow process X_t converges (as \epsilon\to0) to the solution of the averaged ODE

\dot{X} = \bar{h}(X) = \int h(X,y)\,\pi_X(dy).

Theorem A.1 (Borkar 2008, extended by Mahadevan et al. 2025).
Suppose:

· h is Lipschitz in X uniformly in y.
· For each X, Y_t has a uniform spectral gap \rho>0 independent of X.
· The Poisson equation -\mathcal{L}_X u_X(y) = h(X,y)-\bar{h}(X) has a unique solution satisfying polynomial growth bounds.
  Then the interpolated process X^\epsilon(t) = X_{t/\epsilon} converges almost surely to the internally chain‑recurrent set of the ODE \dot{X}=\bar{h}(X).

A.3 Kuramoto Model and Ott–Antonsen Reduction

The classic Kuramoto model for phases \theta_i(t) is

\dot\theta_i = \omega_i + \frac{K}{N}\sum_{j=1}^N \sin(\theta_j-\theta_i).

g(\omega) is the frequency distribution. The order parameter is Re^{i\psi} = \frac1N\sum e^{i\theta_j}. In the thermodynamic limit, the dynamics of R satisfies (for Lorentzian g):

\dot R = \left(\frac{K}{2} - \Delta\right) R - \frac{K}{2} R^3.

The incoherent state R=0 loses stability at K_c=2\Delta.

A.4 Random Matrix Theory – Semicircle Law and Tracy–Widom

For a real symmetric N\times N random matrix with i.i.d. entries of variance 1/N, the empirical spectral density converges weakly to the Wigner semicircle \rho(\lambda) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{4-\lambda^2} on [-2,2]. The largest eigenvalue \lambda_1 fluctuates on scale N^{-2/3}; rescaled, (\lambda_1-2)N^{2/3} follows the Tracy–Widom distribution. The right edge is at 2, and finite‑N corrections to the mean are O(N^{-2/3}).

A.5 Branching Processes and Avalanches

A Galton–Watson branching process with offspring mean m has generating function f(s). If m=1 (critical), the extinction probability is 1, and the total progeny size S satisfies P(S=n) \sim C n^{-3/2} for large n. The branching ratio \sigma_b can be estimated as the ratio of total sizes in consecutive avalanches.

A.6 Contraction Theory

A deterministic system \dot x = f(x) is contracting with respect to a metric M(x) if

\dot V = \frac12 \frac{d}{dt}(x^\top M(x) x) \le -c\, x^\top M(x) x

for some c>0. For delayed systems, a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional is used to prove global exponential stability.

