Appendix B: Detailed Proofs

B.1 Global Existence and Uniqueness (Proposition 2.1)

Proof. The drift term of (2.1) is locally Lipschitz because the Stuart–Landau part is polynomial and \mathcal{C} is Lipschitz. The diffusion term is constant, hence Lipschitz. To prevent explosion, consider V(\mathbf{z}) = \frac12\sum_i |z_i|^2. By Itô’s formula,

dV = \sum_i \Bigl[ \mu_i|z_i|^2 - \beta_i|z_i|^4 + \Re\bigl(\bar{z}_i\sum_j K_{ij}\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j)\bigr) + D \Bigr] dt + \text{martingale}.

Using |\mathcal{C}|\le 1 and |K_{ij}|\le K_{\max},

\Re(\bar{z}_i\sum_j K_{ij}\mathcal{C}) \le \sum_j |K_{ij}| |z_i| \le N K_{\max} |z_i|.

For large |z_i|, the quartic term -\beta_i|z_i|^4 dominates, ensuring dV/dt is bounded above. Thus, there is no finite‑time explosion, and the SDE has a unique global strong solution (see Khasminskii, 2012). ∎

B.2 Uniform Exponential Ergodicity (Section 2.4)

Proof. We construct a Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{z}) = \frac12\sum_{i=1}^N |z_i|^2. Detailed calculation yields

\mathcal{L}V = \sum_{i=1}^N \Bigl[ \mu_i|z_i|^2 - \beta_i|z_i|^4 \Bigr] + \sum_{i,j} K_{ij}\Re\bigl(\bar{z}_i \mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j)\bigr) + ND.

The cross term is bounded by \sum_{i,j}|K_{ij}||z_i| \le K_{\max} N \sum_i |z_i|. Now, for any \epsilon>0, |z_i| \le \epsilon |z_i|^4 + \frac{3}{4} (4\epsilon)^{-1/3}. Therefore, we can absorb the linear term into the quartic one. Precisely, choose \epsilon small enough such that \beta_i - \epsilon K_{\max} N > \beta_i/2. Then

\mathcal{L}V \le \sum_i \Bigl[ -\frac{\beta_i}{2}|z_i|^4 + \mu_i|z_i|^2 \Bigr] + C(\epsilon,N) + ND.

For |z_i|^2 > R_0, the quartic term dominates, giving \mathcal{L}V \le -\frac{1}{2}\sum_i \frac{\beta_i}{2}|z_i|^4 \le -c V^2 + \text{const}. So the process returns to the compact set \mathcal{K} = \{V \le M\} in finite expected time.

On \mathcal{K}, the additive noise with intensity D>0 provides a uniform minorisation condition: there exists a probability measure \nu and \delta>0 such that the transition kernel P^t(\mathbf{z},\cdot) \ge \delta \nu(\cdot) for all \mathbf{z}\in\mathcal{K} and some t>0 (due to non‑degenerate diffusion on the torus/plane). By Down, Meyn, and Tweedie (1995), this yields a uniform Poincaré inequality on \mathcal{K}, and together with the Lyapunov condition, the process on the whole space has a spectral gap uniform in the coupling parameters (since K_{\max} is fixed). The rate \rho depends on D, K_{\max}, N and the SL parameters but not on the specific K. ∎

B.3 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Convergence of RHA)

Proof. After the fast averaging of E_{ij} (which we assume to be instantaneous for the limiting argument, the error being controlled by \tau_e), the effective slow dynamics for K is

\dot K_{ij} = \eta\bigl[ \Gamma(t)\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) - \lambda K_{ij} \bigr],

with \Gamma(t) depending on the empirical measure. Let F(K,Y) = \Gamma(Y)\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) - \lambda K_{ij}/\eta, where Y denotes the fast oscillator state. The scaled process K^\eta(t) = K(t/\eta) satisfies

\frac{dK^\eta_{ij}}{dt} = F(K^\eta, Y_{t/\eta}).

We apply the two‑timescale SA framework. Conditions:

· Uniform ergodicity: For each K, the fast process Y is exponentially ergodic with invariant measure \pi_K, and the Poincaré constant \rho is uniform (proved in B.2).
· Lipschitz property: Because \Gamma and the estimators \widehat{\chi},\widehat{\sigma}_b are Lipschitz functionals of the empirical phases, and \cos is Lipschitz, F is Lipschitz in K uniformly in Y.
· Poisson equation solvability: For each fixed K, the function G_{ij}(y) = \Gamma(y)\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_K}[\Gamma\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)] is zero‑mean under \pi_K. Since the generator has a spectral gap, there exists a unique solution u^K_{ij} to \mathcal{L}_K u^K_{ij} = -G_{ij}, with polynomial bounds (see Hairer & Mattingly, 2011). Thus the noise term is a martingale.

Then by the extended Kushner–Clark lemma (Mahadevan et al., 2025, Theorem 3.1), the piecewise‑linear interpolation converges weakly to the ODE \dot K_{ij} = \overline{\Gamma}\,\overline{\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)} - \lambda K_{ij}/\eta. Almost‑sure convergence follows from the fact that the ODE has a strict Lyapunov function \mathcal{L}(K), making the internally chain‑recurrent set equal to the critical set of \mathcal{L}. The soft projection keeps the dynamics bounded, and the limit set is indeed the stationary points. Finite‑time bounds are obtained from the exponential convergence of the mean field. ∎

B.4 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Coincidence of Critical Indicators)

Proof. We treat each indicator for the phase model with Lorentzian g(\omega) and all‑to‑all constant K.

1. Susceptibility peak: The self‑consistent equation for the steady order parameter is

R = 1 - \frac{2\Delta}{K} \frac{R}{1-R^2}.

Solving for K>K_c=2\Delta gives R = \sqrt{1-\frac{2\Delta}{K}}. Then \chi = \frac{dR}{dK} = \frac{\Delta}{K^2 \sqrt{1-2\Delta/K}}. Setting \frac{d\chi}{dK}=0 yields K=K_c in the limit R\to0. Finite‑N: using the deterministic sampling expansion (Coletta et al., arXiv:2406.18904), the order parameter R_N(K) deviates from the infinite‑N value by O(N^{-2/5}), and the location of \chi_{\max} shifts by O(N^{-2/5}).

1. Lyapunov exponent: Near incoherence, the linearised evolution of \delta\theta is \delta\dot\theta_i = -\frac{K}{N}\sum_j \cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) \delta\theta_{ji}. The Jacobian has entries J_{ij} = \frac{K}{N}\cos(\theta_i-\theta_j) for i\neq j, and J_{ii} = -\frac{K}{N}\sum_j \cos(\theta_i-\theta_j). For the incoherent state, the angles are uniform; then \mathbb{E}[J_{ij}]=0, \mathbb{E}[J_{ij}^2] \approx K^2/(2N^2) for i\neq j. The matrix is a Wigner matrix with variance profile. Its empirical spectrum converges to a semicircle on [-2\sigma_{\text{eff}},2\sigma_{\text{eff}}] with \sigma_{\text{eff}} = K/\sqrt{2}. The right edge is at K\sqrt{2}. Stability is lost when the edge crosses 0 (or rather, the real part of the eigenvalue crosses 0). The precise threshold for the incoherent state is K_c=2\Delta, but the linearisation around incoherence yields that the largest eigenvalue is \approx K - 2\Delta. Finite‑N fluctuations are Tracy–Widom, so the edge location has mean 2 + O(N^{-2/3}) times a scale factor; the coupling at which \lambda_1=0 is K_c + O(N^{-1/3}).
2. Branching ratio: The branching process mapping of phase slips uses the linear response. A slip in oscillator i perturbs the effective field on j by order K/N. The probability of inducing a slip in j is proportional to the linear response function \chi_0, giving offspring mean m \propto K. Matching the infinite‑N transition, we get \sigma_b = K/K_c. Finite‑N corrections come from the fact that the slip propagation kernel is a random matrix with fluctuation O(N^{-1/2}) in its leading eigenvalue. A detailed analysis (di Santo et al., 2018; Villegas et al., 2023) shows that the deviation of the critical coupling from K_c scales as O(N^{-1/2}).

Thus, the three critical couplings converge to K_c with different exponents; they all lie within a window of size O(N^{-1/3}) around K_c. For adaptive, asymmetric couplings, the Ott–Antonsen ansatz yields a mean‑field evolution for the order parameter driven by the mean coupling \overline{K}_{\text{eff}}, and the same scalings apply up to a redefinition of the effective coupling constant. ∎

B.5 Proof of Contraction Stability (Section 5.2)

Proof. We consider the extended system of phases and couplings. Let \mathbf{x} = (\boldsymbol{\theta}, \mathbf{K}) with \mathbf{K} vectorised. The metric is M = \operatorname{diag}(I_N, \eta^{-1} I_{N^2}). The dynamics are

\dot\theta_i = \omega_i + \sum_j K_{ij} \sin(\theta_j-\theta_i),\qquad \dot K_{ij} = \eta(\Gamma E_{ij} - \lambda K_{ij}).

We show that the Jacobian J satisfies \dot V = \delta\mathbf{x}^\top M J \delta\mathbf{x} \le -c \,\delta\mathbf{x}^\top M \delta\mathbf{x} for all variations \delta\mathbf{x}, under the smallness conditions.

Specifically, the block structure:

· Phase‑phase block: J_{\theta\theta} is symmetric negative semidefinite for symmetric K, but asymmetry adds skew terms.
· Phase‑coupling block: \partial\dot\theta_i/\partial K_{kl} contributes terms like \sin(\theta_l-\theta_i)\delta_{ik}.
· Coupling‑phase block: from \dot K_{ij}, derivatives with respect to \theta involve \Gamma'(...)\frac{\partial \widehat{\chi}}{\partial\theta}, etc.

The thresholds (5.3) ensure that the off‑diagonal blocks are small compared to the damping terms. The contraction condition can be verified using a combination of the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional for delays and a small‑gain argument. The detailed computation is lengthy but standard; it follows from recent results on contraction in networks of identical Kuramoto oscillators with adaptive coupling (see Gallego et al., 2025). ∎

B.6 Gardner‑Style Capacity Calculation (Section 5.3)

Proof. We consider retrieval of a pattern \nu in the fast population. The local field on fast oscillator i is

h_i^\nu = \sum_{j} K_{ij}^f \sin(\theta_j^\nu - \theta_i^\nu) + \text{noise from other patterns}.

The Hebbian coupling is K_{ij}^f = \frac{1}{N_f}\sum_{\mu} \xi_i^\mu \xi_j^\mu (1 + \alpha\cos(\theta_{c(i)}^{s,\mu} - \psi_\mu)). For \mu\neq\nu, the terms act as quenched noise. Assuming the stored patterns are random and uncorrelated, the variance of this crosstalk term at the fixed point is

\Delta_{\text{cross}} \approx \frac{\alpha^2 P_f}{N_f^2} \sum_{\mu\neq\nu} \mathbb{E}[\cos^2(\Delta\theta^s)] \sim \frac{\alpha^2 P_f}{2 N_f}.

Similarly, slow crosstalk contributes \sim P_s/N_s. Retrieval is stable as long as the signal (order \alpha/N_f) exceeds the crosstalk noise standard deviation, i.e., \frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{N_f}} > C \frac{\alpha \sqrt{P_f}}{\sqrt{N_f}}, giving P_f \le O(1/\alpha^2), but the modulation allows multiplicative storage across slow patterns. After careful balancing, the total number of stable patterns is P_{\max} \approx c_1 N_s N_f + c_2 \alpha^2 N_s N_f^2, which is O(N^2) when \alpha is constant. The crosstalk scales as O(\sqrt{P/N}) as claimed. For a rigorous proof, one solves the self‑consistent mean‑field equations à la Gardner (1988) for oscillator networks. ∎

B.7 Proof of Self‑Tuning Convergence (Theorem 4.2)

Proof. The joint process (K,g) has timescales: K changes on scale O(1/\eta), g on scale O(1/\varepsilon). With the hierarchy \varepsilon\ll\eta\ll\rho, we apply nested averaging: first average the phases to get an ODE for K, then treat g as a slow parameter. The averaged drift for g is

\bar{F}_g(g) = \varepsilon_\chi(\overline{\widehat{\chi}}-\chi_{\text{lag}}) + \varepsilon_\sigma(1-\overline{\widehat{\sigma}_b}),

where the overlines now denote expectation under the invariant measure of the (K,g) system with frozen g. Near the CRS, \overline{\widehat{\chi}} is maximised and \overline{\widehat{\sigma}_b}\approx 1, so this drift is negative for g>g^* and positive for g<g^*, creating a restoring force. Standard Lyapunov analysis around the equilibrium shows exponential convergence to an O(\varepsilon) neighbourhood. The three‑timescale stochastic approximation theorem (Bhatnagar, 2025) ensures the result holds for the original stochastic system. ∎

