Appendix D: Algorithmic Specification

Even though the RPNI machine is defined in continuous time, for completeness we provide a formal algorithmic description in both differential‑equation form and a possible numerical integration schema to guide future simulation‑based studies (though none are performed in this thesis).

D.1 Continuous‑Time Specification

The system is defined by the following set of ordinary differential and stochastic differential equations.

Oscillator Core:

\frac{d z_i}{d t} = (\mu_i + i\omega_i)z_i - (\beta_i + i\gamma_i)|z_i|^2 z_i + g(t)\sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}(t)\,\mathcal{C}(z_i,z_j) + I_i^{\text{ext}}(t) + \sqrt{2D}\,\xi_i(t),

with \mathcal{C} as in (2.2).

Plasticity Module:

\tau_e \frac{d E_{ij}}{d t} = -E_{ij} + \cos(\theta_i-\theta_j),\qquad \theta_i = \arg(z_i),

\frac{d K_{ij}}{d t} = \eta\,\Gamma(t)\,E_{ij} - \lambda\,K_{ij},\quad \text{projected onto } [-K_{\max},K_{\max}].

Criticality Controller:

\widehat{R}(t) = \text{low‑pass filtered } \bigl|\frac{1}{N}\sum e^{i\theta_i}\bigr|,

\widehat{\chi}(t) = \frac{N}{2D T_{\text{win}}}\int_{t-T_{\text{win}}}^t (\widehat{R}(s)-\bar{\widehat{R}}(t))^2 ds,

\chi_{\text{lag}}(t) = \tau_\chi^{-1} \int_{-\infty}^t e^{-(t-s)/\tau_\chi} \widehat{\chi}(s) ds,

\widehat{\sigma}_b(t) = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{M-1} S_{k+1}}{\sum_{k=1}^M S_k},\quad \text{(from a smoothed avalanche detector)},

\Gamma(t) = \sigma\bigl(\kappa_\chi(\widehat{\chi}-\chi_{\text{lag}})\bigr)\,\sigma\bigl(\kappa_\sigma(1-\widehat{\sigma}_b)\bigr),

\frac{d g}{d t} = \varepsilon_\chi(\widehat{\chi}-\chi_{\text{lag}}) + \varepsilon_\sigma(1-\widehat{\sigma}_b),\quad g(0)=1.

Input Encoding:

I_i^{\text{ext}}(t) = \sum_{k=1}^m W_{ik}^{\text{in}} u_k(t).

Readout:

y_\ell(t) = \sum_{i=1}^N \bigl( w_{\ell i}\cos\theta_i(t) + v_{\ell i}\sin\theta_i(t) \bigr) + b_\ell.

D.2 Discrete‑Time Numerical Schema (for reference)

To simulate the continuous system, one may use an Euler–Maruyama scheme with small step h\ll \tau_e. The algorithm proceeds per time step as:

1. Update phases and amplitudes:
      Compute drift f_i from SL equation using current z and inputs. Update z_i \leftarrow z_i + h f_i + \sqrt{2Dh}\,\mathcal{N}(0,1).
2. Update eligibility trace:
      E_{ij} \leftarrow E_{ij} + \frac{h}{\tau_e}\bigl(-E_{ij} + \cos(\theta_i-\theta_j)\bigr).
3. Update global measures:
      Compute R, update moving averages for \chi, detect phase slips and update avalanche statistics, compute \Gamma(t_n).
4. Update couplings:
      K_{ij} \leftarrow K_{ij} + h(\eta\Gamma E_{ij} - \lambda K_{ij}), then clip to [-K_{\max},K_{\max}].
5. Update global gain:
      g \leftarrow g + h\bigl(\varepsilon_\chi(\widehat{\chi}-\chi_{\text{lag}}) + \varepsilon_\sigma(1-\widehat{\sigma}_b)\bigr).
6. Compute output: y_\ell from current phases.

The parameter values and timescale separations must be respected for convergence.

